  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A Small Nation in the Turmoil of the Second World War shows a strong record of public visibility and sustained use. The book has been mentioned 47 times on Wikipedia, has been downloaded 2,902 times through OAPEN, and has already attracted 2 scholarly citations, indicating an early but real citation footprint alongside broader public reach.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Herman Van der Wee\u2019s book examines Belgium\u2019s monetary and financial history during the Second World War, with attention to the country\u2019s links with Germany, France, the Netherlands, Great Britain, the United States, and Luxembourg. The record shows that this topic has found resonance beyond a narrow specialist audience: Wikipedia mentions point to active public discourse, while the OAPEN download data indicate substantial readership and continued interest in the work as an open-access book.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still modest, but it is already visible. OpenAlex currently records 2 citations, with citing works appearing in 2013 and 2015. That early uptake includes an article in The Journal of Economic History and a chapter in Cambridge University Press eBooks, suggesting that the book has begun to enter scholarly conversations across economic history and broader historical studies.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding citation landscape is interdisciplinary rather than tightly concentrated in one niche. The citing works connect the book to themes such as World War II, political science, economic history, and political economy, and they appear across history and education-linked contexts. This pattern suggests that the book is being used not only for Belgian wartime financial history, but also for comparative discussions of wartime economies and political order.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Availability also supports reach: the book is openly accessible through repository and publisher channels, and the metadata show a hybrid open-access status. Taken together, the record points to a scholarly monograph with clear public visibility, meaningful digital use, and an emerging citation profile that is likely to develop further as it continues to circulate.</p>",
